  "cells": [
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "# 3.02 — Frenet–Serret and Torsion\n\nCurvature told us how fast a curve turns, but not in which direction it turns *next*. A circle and a helix can have the same curvature; what separates them is that the helix keeps escaping the plane it is momentarily turning in.\n\nThe tool is a **moving frame**: an orthonormal basis carried along the curve, adapted to the curve rather than to the ambient axes. For a unit-speed curve with $\\kappa > 0$:\n\n$$T = \\gamma' \\quad(\\text{tangent}), \\qquad N = \\frac{T'}{\\kappa} \\quad(\\text{normal}), \\qquad B = T \\times N \\quad(\\text{binormal}).$$\n\nThese are orthonormal at every point — the **Frenet frame** — and the whole local theory of space curves consists of describing how the frame rotates as you move along.\n\nBecause the frame stays orthonormal, its rate of change is constrained: differentiating $T\\cdot T = 1$, $T \\cdot N = 0$ and so on forces the derivative matrix to be **antisymmetric**. That is exactly the structure met in 1.03, where $\\dot R R^{\\mathsf T}$ was antisymmetric because $R$ stayed orthogonal. In three dimensions an antisymmetric matrix has three independent entries — but one of them vanishes here for a structural reason, leaving just two:\n\n$$\\begin{pmatrix} T' \\\\ N' \\\\ B'\\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 0 & \\kappa & 0 \\\\ -\\kappa & 0 & \\tau \\\\ 0 & -\\tau & 0\\end{pmatrix}\\begin{pmatrix} T \\\\ N \\\\ B\\end{pmatrix}.$$\n\nThese are the **Frenet–Serret equations**, and the new quantity $\\tau$ is the **torsion** — the rate at which the curve twists out of its osculating plane.\n\nThe pay-off is the **fundamental theorem of curves**: $\\kappa$ and $\\tau$, as functions of arc length, determine the curve completely up to a rigid motion. Two numbers per point are the entire local geometry — and this is the exact analogue of 1.07, where pairwise distances determined a configuration up to $E(n)$.",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## 1. Why the matrix is antisymmetric, and why one entry vanishes\n\nWrite the frame as the rows of a matrix $F = (T; N; B)$. Orthonormality says $FF^{\\mathsf T} = I$, and differentiating gives\n\n$$F'F^{\\mathsf T} + F(F')^{\\mathsf T} = 0,$$\n\nso $A = F'F^{\\mathsf T}$ satisfies $A + A^{\\mathsf T} = 0$: **antisymmetric**. This is the identical computation to 1.03 §\"measuring the degeneracy\", and for the identical reason — a frame that stays orthonormal can only rotate, and infinitesimal rotations are antisymmetric. Stage 5 will call $A$ an element of the Lie algebra $\\mathfrak{so}(3)$.\n\nAn antisymmetric $3\\times3$ matrix has three free entries, so a priori\n\n$$A = \\begin{pmatrix} 0 & a & b \\\\ -a & 0 & c \\\\ -b & -c & 0 \\end{pmatrix}.$$\n\nNow identify them:\n\n- $a = T'\\cdot N = \\kappa$, by the definition of $N$.\n- $b = T'\\cdot B = \\kappa N \\cdot B = 0$. **This is the entry that vanishes**, and it does so by construction: $T'$ points along $N$, so it has no $B$-component at all.\n- $c = N'\\cdot B =: \\tau$, the definition of torsion.\n\nSo the two-parameter form is forced. The vanishing of $b$ is not a coincidence but a consequence of choosing $N$ to be the direction $T$ is turning towards.\n\n**Interpretation of $\\tau$.** The plane spanned by $T$ and $N$ is the **osculating plane** — the plane the curve is instantaneously turning in, containing the osculating circle of 3.01. Its normal is $B$, and $B' = -\\tau N$. So\n\n$$\\tau = 0 \\iff B \\text{ is constant} \\iff \\text{the curve is planar}.$$\n\nTorsion measures failure to be flat. A curve with $\\kappa > 0$ and $\\tau \\equiv 0$ lies in a fixed plane; nonzero $\\tau$ is exactly what makes a curve genuinely three-dimensional.\n\n**General formula.** For an arbitrary regular parametrisation,\n\n$$\\tau = \\frac{(\\gamma' \\times \\gamma'')\\cdot \\gamma'''}{|\\gamma'\\times\\gamma''|^2},$$\n\nwith the scalar triple product of 1.01 in the numerator — a signed volume, so $\\tau$, unlike $\\kappa$, carries a sign. Right-handed helices have $\\tau > 0$, left-handed ones $\\tau < 0$: chirality again, and this time it is a local quantity.",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "load(\"numerics\")$\nload(\"ax-plots\")$\nload(\"linearalgebra\")$\nratprint : false$\n\ncross3(u,v) := [u[2]*v[3]-u[3]*v[2], u[3]*v[1]-u[1]*v[3], u[1]*v[2]-u[2]*v[1]]$\nvnorm(u)    := sqrt( ratsimp(u . u) )$\nD(g, var)   := map(lambda([e], diff(e, var)), g)$\nsimpk(e)    := radcan( trigsimp(e) )$\nsimpv(v)    := map(simpk, v)$\n\n/* Frenet frame and the two invariants, for ANY regular parametrisation */\nTvec(g,u) := simpv( D(g,u) / vnorm(D(g,u)) )$\nkappa(g,u):= simpk( vnorm(cross3(D(g,u), D(D(g,u),u))) / vnorm(D(g,u))^3 )$\ntau(g,u)  := block([g1,g2,g3,c],\n  g1 : D(g,u), g2 : D(g1,u), g3 : D(g2,u), c : cross3(g1,g2),\n  simpk( (c . g3) / (c . c) ) )$\nBvec(g,u) := block([c], c : cross3(D(g,u), D(D(g,u),u)),\n  simpv( c / vnorm(c) ) )$\nNvec(g,u) := simpv( cross3(Bvec(g,u), Tvec(g,u)) )$\n\nassume(aa > 0, bb > 0)$\nhelix : [aa*cos(t), aa*sin(t), bb*t]$\n\nprint(\"helix (a cos t, a sin t, b t):\")$\nprint(\"   κ = \", kappa(helix, t))$\nprint(\"   τ = \", tau(helix, t))$\nprint(\"   both constant — the helix is the curve of constant κ and τ\")$\n\n/* The frame is orthonormal */\nTh : Tvec(helix,t)$  Nh : Nvec(helix,t)$  Bh : Bvec(helix,t)$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"frame check:  |T| = \", simpk(vnorm(Th)), \"   |N| = \", simpk(vnorm(Nh)),\n      \"   |B| = \", simpk(vnorm(Bh)))$\nprint(\"   T·N = \", simpk(Th . Nh), \"   T·B = \", simpk(Th . Bh),\n      \"   N·B = \", simpk(Nh . Bh))$\nprint(\"   T×N − B = \", simpv(cross3(Th,Nh) - Bh), \"  (right-handed)\")$\n\n/* Handedness: a left-handed helix has negative torsion */\nhelixL : [aa*cos(t), -aa*sin(t), bb*t]$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"left-handed helix:  κ = \", kappa(helixL,t), \"   τ = \", tau(helixL,t))$\nprint(\"   same curvature, opposite torsion — torsion detects chirality\")$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 1,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "name": "stdout",
          "output_type": "stream",
          "text": [
            "helix (a cos t, a sin t, b t):\n   κ =  aa/(bb^2+aa^2)\n   τ =  bb/(bb^2+aa^2)\n   both constant — the helix is the curve of constant κ and τ\nframe check:  |T| =  1    |N| =  1    |B| =  1\n   T·N =  0    T·B =  0    N·B =  0\n   T×N − B =  [0,0,0]   (right-handed)\nleft-handed helix:  κ =  aa/(bb^2+aa^2)    τ =  -(bb/(bb^2+aa^2))\n   same curvature, opposite torsion — torsion detects chirality"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "/* Verify the Frenet–Serret equations.  Derivatives are with respect to ARC\n   LENGTH, so d/ds = (1/v) d/dt where v is the speed. */\nvh : simpk( vnorm(D(helix,t)) )$\ndds(f) := simpv( D(f,t) / vh )$\n\nkh : kappa(helix,t)$  th : tau(helix,t)$\n\nprint(\"speed v = \", vh)$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"T' − κN      = \", simpv( dds(Th) - kh*Nh ))$\nprint(\"N' + κT − τB = \", simpv( dds(Nh) + kh*Th - th*Bh ))$\nprint(\"B' + τN      = \", simpv( dds(Bh) + th*Nh ))$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"all zero — the Frenet–Serret equations hold ∎\")$\n\n/* The derivative matrix really is antisymmetric */\nFm : matrix(Th, Nh, Bh)$\nAm : simpk( matrix(dds(Th), dds(Nh), dds(Bh)) . transpose(Fm) )$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"A = F' Fᵀ = \", Am)$\nprint(\"A + Aᵀ    = \", simpk(Am + transpose(Am)), \"  — antisymmetric ✓\")$\nprint(\"entry (1,3) = \", Am[1][3], \"  — the structurally forced zero\")$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 2,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "name": "stdout",
          "output_type": "stream",
          "text": [
            "speed v =  sqrt(bb^2+aa^2)\nT' − κN      =  [0,0,0]\nN' + κT − τB =  [0,0,0]\nB' + τN      =  [0,0,0]\nall zero — the Frenet–Serret equations hold ∎\nA = F' Fᵀ =\n            matrix([0,aa/(bb^2+aa^2),0],[-(aa/(bb^2+aa^2)),0,bb/(bb^2+aa^2)],\n                   [0,-(bb/(bb^2+aa^2)),0])\nA + Aᵀ    =  matrix([0,0,0],[0,0,0],[0,0,0])   — antisymmetric ✓\nentry (1,3) =  0   — the structurally forced zero"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## 2. The fundamental theorem of curves\n\n> **Theorem.** Given smooth functions $\\kappa(s) > 0$ and $\\tau(s)$ on an interval, there exists a unit-speed curve with those invariants, and it is **unique up to a rigid motion of $\\mathbb R^3$**.\n\nBoth halves follow from the theory of ODEs rather than from geometry.\n\n**Existence.** The Frenet–Serret equations are a linear ODE system for the nine components of $(T,N,B)$, with coefficients built from $\\kappa$ and $\\tau$. Linear systems with smooth coefficients have solutions; and because the coefficient matrix is antisymmetric, an initially orthonormal frame *stays* orthonormal — the antisymmetry is exactly the condition preserving $FF^{\\mathsf T} = I$. Then $\\gamma(s) = \\int T$ recovers the curve.\n\n**Uniqueness.** The solution is determined by its initial data: a starting point $\\gamma(0)$ and a starting frame $(T,N,B)(0)$. But a point plus a right-handed orthonormal frame is precisely the data of an element of $SE(3)$ — and changing it applies a rigid motion. So the curve is unique up to $SE(3)$.\n\nThis mirrors 1.07 exactly. There, pairwise distances determined a point configuration up to $E(n)$; here, two functions determine a curve up to $SE(3)$. In both cases a complete set of *invariants* has been found, and the ambiguity left over is precisely the group.\n\n**The demonstration below** takes constant $\\kappa = \\tau = \\tfrac12$, integrates the Frenet system numerically from an initial frame, and checks that the reconstructed curve is the helix $(\\cos s/\\sqrt2, \\sin s/\\sqrt2, s/\\sqrt2)$ — which indeed has $\\kappa = \\tau = 1/2$ by the formulas above with $a = b = 1$.\n\nWe integrate with a hand-written fourth-order Runge–Kutta rather than a library routine, so that nothing about the method is hidden.",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "/* Reconstruct a curve from κ and τ by integrating the Frenet system.\n   State yv = [γ(3), T(3), N(3), B(3)] — twelve numbers.\n   (Named yv, not y: a variable called y would later collide with plot2d's\n   [y, min, max] range specification.) */\nkap : 1/2$   tor : 1/2$\n\nfrenet_rhs(u) := block([g,T_,N_,B_],\n  g  : [u[1],u[2],u[3]],   T_ : [u[4],u[5],u[6]],\n  N_ : [u[7],u[8],u[9]],   B_ : [u[10],u[11],u[12]],\n  append( T_,                       /* γ' = T            */\n          kap*N_,                   /* T' = κN           */\n          -kap*T_ + tor*B_,         /* N' = −κT + τB     */\n          -tor*N_ ) )$              /* B' = −τN          */\n\nrk4step(u, hh) := block([k1,k2,k3,k4],\n  k1 : frenet_rhs(u),\n  k2 : frenet_rhs(u + (hh/2)*k1),\n  k3 : frenet_rhs(u + (hh/2)*k2),\n  k4 : frenet_rhs(u + hh*k3),\n  u + (hh/6)*(k1 + 2*k2 + 2*k3 + k4) )$\n\n/* Initial data matching the helix (cos t, sin t, t) at t = 0, where s = √2 t */\nr2 : float(sqrt(2))$\nyv0 : float([ 1,0,0,  0,1/r2,1/r2,  -1,0,0,  0,-1/r2,1/r2 ])$\n\n/* The exact helix, as a function of arc length s */\nexact(s) := float([ cos(s/r2), sin(s/r2), s/r2 ])$\n\nprint(\"initial frame orthonormal?\")$\nprint(\"   |T| = \", float(sqrt(yv0[4]^2+yv0[5]^2+yv0[6]^2)),\n      \"   |N| = \", float(sqrt(yv0[7]^2+yv0[8]^2+yv0[9]^2)),\n      \"   |B| = \", float(sqrt(yv0[10]^2+yv0[11]^2+yv0[12]^2)))$\n\nh : float(2*%pi*r2/2000)$\nyv : yv0$  path : [[yv[1],yv[2],yv[3]]]$\nfor i : 1 thru 2000 do ( yv : rk4step(yv, h),\n                         path : cons([yv[1],yv[2],yv[3]], path) )$\npath : reverse(path)$\n\nsend : float(2000*h)$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"after integrating to s = \", send, \" (one full turn):\")$\nprint(\"   reconstructed γ = \", [yv[1],yv[2],yv[3]])$\nprint(\"   exact helix     = \", exact(send))$\nprint(\"   error           = \", float(sqrt( (yv[1]-exact(send)[1])^2\n                                          + (yv[2]-exact(send)[2])^2\n                                          + (yv[3]-exact(send)[3])^2 )))$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"frame still orthonormal after 2000 steps?\")$\nprint(\"   |T| = \", float(sqrt(yv[4]^2+yv[5]^2+yv[6]^2)),\n      \"   |N| = \", float(sqrt(yv[7]^2+yv[8]^2+yv[9]^2)),\n      \"   |B| = \", float(sqrt(yv[10]^2+yv[11]^2+yv[12]^2)))$\nprint(\"   — antisymmetry of the coefficient matrix preserves orthonormality\")$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 6,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "name": "stdout",
          "output_type": "stream",
          "text": [
            "initial frame orthonormal?\n   |T| =  0.9999999999999999    |N| =  1.0    |B| =  0.9999999999999999\nafter integrating to s =  8.885765876316732  (one full turn):\n   reconstructed γ =\n                     [0.9999999999999892,-5.1010064228140806e-12,\n                      6.283185307179335]\n   exact helix     =  [1.0,-2.4492935982947064e-16,6.283185307179586]\n   error           =  5.106962195769899e-12\nframe still orthonormal after 2000 steps?\n   |T| =  0.9999999999999939    |N| =  0.9999999999999892    |B| =\n         0.9999999999999939\n   — antisymmetry of the coefficient matrix preserves orthonormality"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "/* --- Torsion vanishes exactly for planar curves --- */\nprint(\"τ = 0 characterises planarity:\")$\n\n/* an ellipse in the xy-plane */\nprint(\"   ellipse in z = 0        : τ = \", tau([3*cos(t), 2*sin(t), 0], t))$\n\n/* the same ellipse tilted into a slanted plane x + y + z = 0 */\ntilted : [3*cos(t), 2*sin(t), -3*cos(t) - 2*sin(t)]$\nprint(\"   ellipse in x+y+z = 0    : τ = \", tau(tilted, t))$\nprint(\"     (still planar, so still zero — τ does not notice the tilt)\")$\n\n/* a genuinely non-planar curve */\ntwisted : [t, t^2, t^3]$\nprint(\"   twisted cubic (t,t²,t³) : τ = \", tau(twisted, t), \"  — nonzero\")$\nprint(\"   its κ                   = \", kappa(twisted, t))$\n\n/* Sanity: the twisted cubic's binormal is NOT constant */\nprint(\"\")$\nBt : Bvec(twisted, t)$\nprint(\"   binormal at t = 0 : \", simpv(subst(t=0, Bt)))$\nprint(\"   binormal at t = 1 : \", float(subst(t=1, Bt)))$\nprint(\"   — the osculating plane genuinely turns, which is what τ ≠ 0 means\")$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 4,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "name": "stdout",
          "output_type": "stream",
          "text": [
            "τ = 0 characterises planarity:\n   ellipse in z = 0        : τ =  0\n   ellipse in x+y+z = 0    : τ =  0\n     (still planar, so still zero — τ does not notice the tilt)\n   twisted cubic (t,t²,t³) : τ =  3/(9*t^4+9*t^2+1)   — nonzero\n   its κ                   =  (2*sqrt(9*t^4+9*t^2+1))/(9*t^4+4*t^2+1)^(3/2)\n   binormal at t = 0 :  [0,0,1]\n   binormal at t = 1 :\n                       [0.6882472016116852,-0.6882472016116852,\n                        0.22941573387056174]\n   — the osculating plane genuinely turns, which is what τ ≠ 0 means"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "/* Reconstructed curve against the exact helix, and the error along the way */\nss    : makelist(float(k*h), k, 0, 2000)$\nrecon : path$\nexs   : map(exact, ss)$\nerrs  : makelist( float(sqrt( (recon[k][1]-exs[k][1])^2\n                            + (recon[k][2]-exs[k][2])^2\n                            + (recon[k][3]-exs[k][3])^2 )), k, 1, 2001)$\nprint(\"maximum error over the whole turn = \", lmax(errs))$\n\nplot2d([ [discrete, map(first, recon),  map(second, recon)],\n         [discrete, map(first, exs),    map(second, exs)] ],\n       [x, -1.4, 1.4], [y, -1.4, 1.4],\n       [style, [lines,4],[lines,1]],\n       [legend, \"reconstructed from κ, τ\", \"exact helix\"],\n       [same_xy, true],\n       [title, \"curve rebuilt from its curvature and torsion alone (xy-projection)\"])$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 9,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "name": "stdout",
          "output_type": "stream",
          "text": [
            "maximum error over the whole turn =  5.106962195769899e-12"
          ]
        },
        {
          "data": {
            "image/svg+xml": [
