    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## Summary\n\n$$\\begin{pmatrix} T' \\\\ N' \\\\ B'\\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 0 & \\kappa & 0 \\\\ -\\kappa & 0 & \\tau \\\\ 0 & -\\tau & 0\\end{pmatrix}\\begin{pmatrix} T \\\\ N \\\\ B\\end{pmatrix}$$\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| $\\kappa$ | rate of turning; $> 0$ by convention |\n| $\\tau$ | rate of twisting out of the osculating plane; signed |\n| $\\tau = 0$ | $\\iff$ the curve is planar |\n| $\\kappa, \\tau$ constant | $\\iff$ the curve is a helix (or circle if $\\tau = 0$) |\n| general $\\tau$ | $\\dfrac{(\\gamma'\\times\\gamma'')\\cdot\\gamma'''}{|\\gamma'\\times\\gamma''|^2}$ |\n\nVerified: the helix has $\\kappa = a/(a^2+b^2)$, $\\tau = b/(a^2+b^2)$; its Frenet frame is orthonormal and right-handed; all three Frenet–Serret equations hold identically; $F'F^{\\mathsf T}$ came out as exactly the predicted antisymmetric matrix with the structurally forced zero in position $(1,3)$; a left-handed helix has the same $\\kappa$ and opposite $\\tau$; planar curves have $\\tau = 0$ whether or not their plane is tilted; and integrating the Frenet system from constant $\\kappa = \\tau = \\tfrac12$ reproduced the helix to within $5\\times10^{-12}$ over a full turn, with the frame remaining orthonormal to $10^{-14}$.\n\n## Two things to carry forward\n\n**Orthonormal frame ⟹ antisymmetric derivative.** This has now appeared three times: as $\\dot R R^{\\mathsf T}$ in 1.03, as the constraint forcing $\\mathfrak{so}(3)$, and here as the shape of the Frenet matrix. It is the reason the local theory of curves has exactly *two* invariants rather than three — the third slot is forced to zero by the choice of $N$. In Stage 5 this becomes the general statement that a connection on an orthonormal frame bundle takes values in $\\mathfrak{so}(n)$, and the moving-frame method generalises into Cartan's structure equations.\n\n**A complete set of invariants, modulo a group.** $(\\kappa, \\tau)$ determine a curve up to $SE(3)$, just as pairwise distances determined a configuration up to $E(n)$ in 1.07. This is the recurring shape of every classification in geometry: find quantities unchanged by the group, prove they are complete, and the leftover ambiguity *is* the group.\n\nSurfaces will need more than two numbers — the analogous role is played by the first and second fundamental forms, and the \"fundamental theorem of surfaces\" requires compatibility conditions (the Gauss and Codazzi equations of 3.06) that have no counterpart for curves. Curves are easy precisely because there is nothing for the invariants to be inconsistent about.\n\n## Next\n\n**3.03 — Surfaces and the First Fundamental Form** moves up a dimension. A surface carries a metric inherited from $\\mathbb R^3$ — the first fundamental form — and everything measurable *within* the surface, without reference to the surrounding space, is computed from it.\n\n## References\n\n- do Carmo, M.P. (1976) *Differential Geometry of Curves and Surfaces*. Englewood Cliffs: Prentice-Hall. §1-5 for the Frenet formulas and the fundamental theorem of curves.\n- Pressley, A. (2010) *Elementary Differential Geometry*. 2nd edn. Springer Undergraduate Mathematics Series. London: Springer. Chapter 2, with a careful treatment of the $\\kappa > 0$ hypothesis and what goes wrong at inflection points.\n- ★ Callahan, J.J. (2000) *The Geometry of Spacetime*. New York: Springer. doi:10.1007/978-1-4757-6736-0. Chapter 5 builds surface theory by generalising the curve case, following the same route as this stage.\n- Spivak, M. (1999) *A Comprehensive Introduction to Differential Geometry*, Vol. 2. 3rd edn. Houston: Publish or Perish. Chapter 1 for moving frames, and the historical route from Frenet–Serret to Cartan.\n\nThe equations are due to Jean Frédéric Frenet (1847) and Joseph Alfred Serret (1851), who found them independently.\n\n**A note on the $\\kappa > 0$ hypothesis.** The Frenet frame is undefined where $\\kappa = 0$, since $N = T'/\\kappa$ divides by zero — at an inflection point the curve is not turning and there is no preferred normal. Torsion is likewise undefined there. Curves with isolated zeros of $\\kappa$ therefore need care, and this is the reason the fundamental theorem is stated for $\\kappa > 0$.\n\n---\n*Marked ★ = in the local indexed library (`maths` collection); the rest are standard references given from knowledge, not consulted here. Every code cell in this notebook was executed and its output is the real result; the prose is written from knowledge and checked against these sources where they cover the material.*",
      "metadata": {}
    }
  ]
}